      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-182460",
      ":question/problem": "Given that ${\u2663, \u2661}^{N}$ denotes the set of all infinite sequences with elements from ${\u2663, \u2661}$, complete the following reasoning:\n\nFor any subset $A$ of the set $N$, consider the function $f$ that maps $A$ into the sequence $(s_{1}, s_{2}, s_{3}, \u2026) \u2208 {\u2663, \u2661}^{N}$ such that:\n$1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}}\nSince $f$ is a bijection from {{field-5}} onto ${\u2663, \u2661}^{N}$, we have that\n$\\mid {\u2663, \u2661}^{N}|=$ {{field-6}}."
    },
